# "passion" means a movement of the sensitive appetite throughout: as subject matter in S1, as what is absent in C1, and as what the overflow produces in C.
# The joy in the will is explicitly not a passion. Its overflow into the sensitive appetite is what becomes passion, so the text marks this distinction itself rather than shifting senses.
# A2 moves from justice to "a virtue" in general. That is a widening of scope, not a change in what "passion" or "perfect" means.
